<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the three helper binaries bundled in Contents/MacOS: kagisecure-mcp,
  kagisecure-nmhost and the kagisecure CLI (ADR-0026).

  They are deliberately as close to empty as a file can be. Each helper is signed under the
  Hardened Runtime, and none of them needs a single one of the exceptions the Hardened Runtime
  exists to grant: no JIT, no writable+executable memory, no unsigned or dynamically loaded
  libraries, no DYLD environment variables, no disabled library validation. Every dependency is
  statically linked into the binary.

  App Sandbox is stated rather than omitted, for the same reason ADR-0010 states it on the app: a
  reader should see a decision, not an oversight. The sidecar talks to the app over a Unix-domain
  socket at a documented path outside any container, and `kagisecure run` spawns a program the
  user names — neither is something a sandboxed process does.

  What is NOT here matters as much as what is:

  * `com.apple.security.get-task-allow` — Apple rejects it at notarization. A Debug build carries
    it automatically, which is why the release pipeline builds Release and asserts its absence
    from every Mach-O before it submits anything (`cargo xtask dist`).
  * `keychain-access-groups` — a restricted entitlement AMFI validates against a provisioning
    profile at every exec, so a binary carrying it without one is SIGKILLed before main. See
    ADR-0011 and ADR-0025. No helper needs the keychain.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
